# Ignore rules scoped to the curl-urlapi-rs crate.
#
# Purpose: after a full crate build and a parity run, "git status --porcelain"
# must report nothing at all for this directory. Only the artifact classes the
# repository root .gitignore does not already reach are listed below. The root
# file is out of scope for this work and is not modified.
#
# Every pattern here, and every omission recorded at the end of this file, was
# established by running "git check-ignore -v" against the root file rather
# than by reading it. Anchoring is what makes that necessary: a root entry
# spelled with a leading slash never reaches a subdirectory, while one spelled
# without a slash matches at every depth. Both cases occur below.

# Cargo's output directory. Holds libcurl_urlapi_rs.a, libcurl_urlapi_rs.so,
# the rlib, the crate's test binaries and every build intermediate. The root
# file ignores archives and object files by extension, but neither the
# directory itself nor the many extensionless intermediates inside it, so this
# pattern is required. The trailing slash keeps it to directories.
target/

# Scratch directory for the parity workflow driven from scripts/. Holds the
# reference libcurl archive with urlapi.c.o deleted, the symlink to
# tests/libtest/lib1560.c, the harness shim header, and the harness and demo
# binaries for both link modes.
#
# The root file does carry a build entry, but it is spelled "/build/" with a
# leading slash, which anchors it to the repository root and therefore never
# reaches this subdirectory. Dropping the pattern below on the strength of
# that root entry would leave the entire scratch tree reported as untracked.
# The trailing slash keeps this from matching the build.rs script.
build/

# Shared libraries produced by the cdylib crate type, including the versioned
# names that cargo-c emits. The root file ignores *.dll for Windows but has no
# rule for ELF or Mach-O shared objects anywhere.
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib

# The pkg-config file cargo-c emits alongside them. The root file ignores the
# literal name libcurl.pc only, not this crate's.
*.pc

# Source-based coverage artifacts. rust-toolchain.toml provisions
# llvm-tools-preview, so a coverage run is expected here, and these two
# patterns are required rather than precautionary: measured, a
# RUSTFLAGS="-C instrument-coverage" cargo test writes its raw profiles into
# the PACKAGE ROOT -- this directory -- and not into target/, because that is
# the working directory Cargo gives a test binary. Verified with
# "git check-ignore -v": before these lines, a default_*.profraw here was
# reported by "git status --porcelain" as untracked, which is exactly the state
# the purpose note at the top of this file rules out. .profdata is listed with
# it because llvm-profdata merge writes wherever it is told, and the same
# directory is the obvious place.
*.profraw
*.profdata

# Deliberately NOT listed above, so that each omission reads as a decision
# rather than an oversight. Line numbers refer to the root .gitignore.
#
# *.a         already ignored, root line 11.
# *.o         already ignored, root line 14.
# Makefile    already ignored, root line 35. That entry carries no slash, so
#             unlike "/build/" it matches at every depth: a file named
#             Makefile in this directory would be silently untracked. That is
#             precisely why the build driver here is named GNUmakefile, a name
#             GNU make prefers anyway, so a bare "make" still works. The entry
#             is deliberately not re-enabled with a "!Makefile" negation,
#             which would change repository-wide behavior to suit one
#             directory.
# Cargo.lock  intentionally tracked, never ignored. Library crates often
#             ignore their lockfile; this one commits it so the resolved
#             dependency versions stay pinned and reproducible.
